  <p class="kicker">{{ site.researchByUrl[page.url].kicker }}</p>
  <h1>The Zero-Seam Test</h1>
  <p class="lede muted">A research proposal for asking whether JWST&rsquo;s little red dots are fully explained
    by young black holes, dense gas and rapidly assembling hosts&mdash;or whether their population retains a
    measurable signature of an integration front carrying inherited cosmic residue.</p>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame frame--first illuminated">
    <p class="kicker">Proposal abstract</p>
    <h2 id="r-abstract">Test the Additional Claim, Not the Metaphor</h2>
    <p>Little red dots are compact sources in the early universe whose spectra increasingly support accreting
      black holes embedded in dense gas. TTI accepts that astrophysical account. Its additional conjecture is
      that some objects are positive-side integration fronts, and that those fronts may be seeded by a global
      residue represented on an orientation-reversed interval \(-1&lt;\xi&lt;0\).</p>
    <p>This programme would not search a telescope image for &ldquo;another universe&rdquo;. It would first turn
      the conjecture into a low-parameter generative model, then test whether that model predicts a
      preregistered combination of abundance, central-to-host growth, environment, energy balance and&mdash;if
      resolvable&mdash;orientation better than conventional astrophysical models. The decisive evidence would
      be successful prediction in data withheld by field and by observing programme, not an improved fit to
      the sample used to invent the model.</p>
    <p class="lede">The null result is valuable: if the crossing law cannot be derived, conserved and made
      predictive before the held-out data are opened, the inherited-residue account is not yet a scientific
      explanation.</p>
    <p class="muted small"><span class="sci">&#9670; observations and standard methods</span> &middot;
      <span class="spec">&#9670; proposed TTI mechanism</span>. No current observation establishes a previous
      universe, a physical negative interval or transfer across zero.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap">
  <div class="page-head tight">
    <p class="kicker">The programme at a glance</p>
    <h2 id="r-summary">A Two-Phase, Twenty-Four-Month Study</h2>
  </div>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="ledger ledger--prose">
      <tbody>
        <tr><th>Primary question</th><td>Does one fixed crossing model predict held-out little-red-dot data
          better than a conventional black-hole, gas and host model?</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Phase I</th><td>Derivation, preregistration and analysis of public archival observations; no new
          telescope time required.</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Phase II</th><td>A prospective, matched follow-up of candidates selected before their decisive
          measurements are examined.</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Primary outcome</th><td>Out-of-sample predictive score on field-level held-out data, reported
          beside calibration and posterior-predictive diagnostics.</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Duration</th><td>Twenty-four months, with stop/go gates after formal derivation and archival
          validation.</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Open outputs</th><td>Preregistration, selection function, catalogue, simulation code, model code,
          negative results and a final decision report.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="divider">✦</div>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame">
    <p class="kicker">The hypotheses</p>
    <h2 id="r-hypotheses">Three Models Must Enter, and Any May Lose</h2>
    <p><strong>\(H_{\rm A}\): astrophysical baseline.</strong> A little red dot is generated by ordinary
      structure formation, black-hole seeding and growth, accretion, dense-gas radiative transfer and stellar
      host assembly. Flexible nuisance parameters are allowed, but their priors and complexity penalty must be
      fixed before the test set is opened. <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></p>
    <p><strong>\(H_{\chi}\): local integration front.</strong> The same physical components are present,
      with one additional local \(\chi\)-evolution law that predicts when a compact source leads the growth of
      its luminous host. There is no inherited negative-side source. <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>
    <p><strong>\(H_{-+}\): inherited-residue front.</strong> The local \(\chi\)-front is driven or biased by
      one global residue projected from \(-1&lt;\xi&lt;0\) through a fixed crossing kernel. This model must
      improve prediction by more than the penalty for its additional structure. <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      H_{\rm A}\subset H_{\chi}\subset H_{-+},
      \qquad
      \text{comparison target: }p(D_{\rm held\ out}\mid H),
      \text{ not }p(D_{\rm training}\mid H).
    \]</p>
    <p>The nesting prevents a common escape. If an inherited-residue prediction fails while the local
      integration model survives, the result supports neither a previous universe nor the \((-1,0)\) sector.
      If both additions fail, the conventional model remains the benchmark.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap">
  <div class="page-head tight">
    <p class="kicker">Four research aims</p>
    <h2 id="r-aims">From Mathematical Admissibility to Blind Prediction</h2>
  </div>
  <div class="grid two">
    <div class="frame card illuminated">
      <h3>Aim 1 &mdash; Derive the crossing law</h3>
      <p>Specify the negative-side state space, its measure, the seam identification and a finite-dimensional
        kernel \(\mathcal K_{-+}\). Derive the allowed coupling to \(\chi\) from one action or junction rule.
        No source-specific free parameter may be introduced.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="frame card">
      <h3>Aim 2 &mdash; Build a reproducible census</h3>
      <p>Construct a selection-aware catalogue from public JWST imaging and spectroscopy, with matched compact
        AGN and galaxy controls at comparable redshift, luminosity and data quality. Publish inclusion,
        exclusion and uncertainty rules.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="frame card">
      <h3>Aim 3 &mdash; Test population predictions</h3>
      <p>Fit all three hypotheses only to training fields. Compare their predictions for abundance, black-hole
        and host growth, compactness, spectral state, environment and energy accounting in untouched fields.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="frame card illuminated">
      <h3>Aim 4 &mdash; Make a prospective forecast</h3>
      <p>Use the archival result to select a small matched follow-up sample and publish object-level forecasts
        before the decisive observations are reduced. This is the main protection against retrospective
        pattern-finding.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame illuminated">
    <p class="kicker">The minimum mathematical object</p>
    <h2 id="r-model">A Crossing Kernel with No Object-by-Object Escape Hatch</h2>
    <p>Let \(r_-(\xi,y)\) be the global residue state and let \(J_+(x,t)\) be its proposed positive-side
      source. The research model begins with</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      J_+(x,t;\phi)=g\!\int_{-1}^{0}\!d\xi\int d\mu_-(y)\,
      \mathcal K_{-+}(x,t;\xi,y\mid\phi)\,r_-(\xi,y),
    \]</p>
    <p>where \(\phi\) is one population-level parameter vector shared by every source. The local integration
      field obeys</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      \frac{D\chi}{Dt}=F_{\rm TTI}[\chi,g_{\mu\nu},\rho,T]+J_+(x,t;\phi),
      \qquad
      \Sigma_{\rm LRD}=\left\{x:\frac12&lt;\chi&lt;1,
      \frac{D\chi}{Dt}>0,|\nabla\chi|>\chi_{\rm c}\right\}.
    \]</p>
    <p>The theory group must map \(\phi\) to observables before fitting. A schematic generative layer is</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      \mathbf y_i
      =\mathcal A(\mathbf x_i;\theta)
      +\mathcal B[J_+(x_i,t_i;\phi)]
      +\boldsymbol\epsilon_i,
    \]</p>
    <p>where \(\mathcal A\) is the conventional astrophysical model, \(\mathbf x_i\) contains measured
      conditions and selection variables, and \(\mathbf y_i\) contains the preregistered outcomes. The
      operator \(\mathcal B\) cannot be replaced by a separate latent adjustment for every little red dot;
      doing so would make \(H_{-+}\) unfalsifiable.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap">
  <div class="page-head tight">
    <p class="kicker">Observable consequences</p>
    <h2 id="r-observables">Five Signatures, One Declared Primary Test</h2>
    <p class="lede muted">No single red spectrum is diagnostic. The proposal lives or dies through a
      constrained pattern across the population.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="grid two">
    <div class="frame card illuminated">
      <h3>1. The inheritance clock</h3>
      <p>The crossing solution must predict a redshift-dependent source term and therefore an onset, peak and
        decline in little-red-dot abundance after the documented selection function is applied. An arbitrary
        curve fitted to the observed decline does not count.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="frame card">
      <h3>2. Central-to-host lag</h3>
      <p>The same clock must predict how black-hole mass, compact-source luminosity and extended host light
        approach one another with cosmic time. This tests the claim that a closure-ready centre precedes its
        luminous host.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="frame card">
      <h3>3. Shared-residue covariance</h3>
      <p>One global residue sampled many times should impose a low-rank relation among source mass, host
        deficit, size, duty state and environment after redshift and selection are controlled. The covariance
        and its sign must be fixed on training data.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="frame card illuminated">
      <h3>4. A closed energy audit</h3>
      <p>An information-only model predicts no unexplained energy excess. A mass-energy-transfer model must
        predict one and balance it through \(J_{-+}^{\nu}\). Accretion luminosity may not be counted again as
        seam luminosity.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="frame card">
      <h3>5. Orientation, only if resolvable</h3>
      <p>An orientation-reversing kernel may predict a parity or handedness statistic in resolved hosts or
        outflows. Because most dots are barely resolved and ordinary selection can mimic asymmetry, this is a
        secondary test unless image simulations demonstrate adequate recovery.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="frame card illuminated">
      <h3>Primary test</h3>
      <p>The preregistered primary endpoint is the joint held-out predictive score for the inheritance clock,
        central-to-host lag and shared covariance. Energy and orientation remain mandatory consistency tests,
        but cannot rescue a failed primary result.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="divider">✦</div>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame">
    <p class="kicker">Data and measurement plan</p>
    <h2 id="r-data">Begin with Public Observations, Then Earn New Telescope Time</h2>
    <p><strong>Imaging.</strong> Use public JWST multi-band imaging to measure compact-source flux, colour,
      effective radius, extended host fraction and local environment with injection-and-recovery tests.
      Stacked measurements may inform population parameters but may not be assigned to individual objects.</p>
    <p><strong>Spectroscopy.</strong> Use public spectra with reliable redshifts to measure line ratios,
      widths, asymmetries, continuum shape and dense-gas indicators. Broadening by electron scattering,
      kinematics and instrumental response must be compared before any residual is assigned to TTI.</p>
    <p><strong>External constraints.</strong> Where available, use X-ray, submillimetre, radio or lensing data
      to constrain obscuration, gas, star formation and mass. Missing coverage is modelled as missingness, not
      as a non-detection.</p>
    <p><strong>Controls.</strong> Match little red dots to compact AGN and galaxies by redshift, depth,
      magnification, luminosity and usable wavelength coverage. The control pipeline must be identical to the
      target pipeline.</p>
    <p><strong>Sample size.</strong> Fix the final archival and prospective sample sizes through mock-catalogue
      recovery simulations before opening held-out fields. The power calculation must include field variance,
      contamination, incompleteness and correlated measurement error; a convenient round number is not a
      design.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap">
  <div class="page-head tight">
    <p class="kicker">Analysis discipline</p>
    <h2 id="r-method">How the Study Avoids Finding What It Put In</h2>
  </div>
  <div class="grid three">
    <div class="frame card illuminated">
      <h3>Split by field</h3>
      <p>Entire survey fields or observing programmes, not random objects, are withheld. This limits leakage
        from shared depths, calibrations and large-scale structure.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="frame card">
      <h3>Forward-model selection</h3>
      <p>Each hypothesis generates intrinsic populations that pass through the same detection, colour,
        morphology and spectroscopic-selection model before comparison with the catalogue.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="frame card illuminated">
      <h3>Penalise flexibility</h3>
      <p>Report held-out log predictive density, calibration and posterior-predictive checks. More parameters
        do not win merely because they improve the training fit.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="frame card">
      <h3>Blind the decisive labels</h3>
      <p>For the prospective phase, freeze candidate selection, model code and forecasts before the decisive
        host or spectral measurements are revealed to the modelling team.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="frame card illuminated">
      <h3>Run null injections</h3>
      <p>Analyse simulated universes containing only conventional black holes and gas. The pipeline must not
        manufacture a residue signature from selection effects or model mismatch.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="frame card">
      <h3>Publish every branch</h3>
      <p>Report the information-only, energy-transfer and junction-only versions separately. The study may not
        switch between them after seeing which observable is inconvenient.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame illuminated">
    <p class="kicker">Conservation gate</p>
    <h2 id="r-conservation">Choose What Crosses Before Counting What Shines</h2>
    <p>The proposal requires one of three mutually distinguishable mechanisms:</p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Information or topology only:</strong> the residue changes boundary conditions, while all
        measurable mass-energy is assembled locally.</li>
      <li><strong>Mass-energy exchange:</strong> the crossing current obeys
        \(\nabla_\mu T_+^{\mu\nu}=J_{-+}^{\nu}\) and
        \(\nabla_\mu T_-^{\mu\nu}=-J_{-+}^{\nu}\), with a defined global accounting surface.</li>
      <li><strong>Junction only:</strong> nothing propagates between sectors; both satisfy one global boundary
        condition that correlates their solutions.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>Failure to choose is a stop condition. In particular, a negative coordinate does not imply negative
      mass, and an unexplained luminosity cannot be called accretion in one calculation and inherited energy
      in another.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap">
  <div class="page-head tight">
    <p class="kicker">Work plan and gates</p>
    <h2 id="r-workplan">Twenty-Four Months with Permission to Stop Early</h2>
  </div>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="ledger ledger--prose">
      <thead><tr><th>Period</th><th>Work package</th><th>Required deliverable</th><th>Decision gate</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Months 1&ndash;4</td><td>Theory and dimensions</td><td>Action or junction rule, kernel family,
          conservation branch and observable map.</td><td>Stop \(H_{-+}\) if no finite, conserved model can be
          written.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Months 3&ndash;8</td><td>Catalogue and simulations</td><td>Selection function, controls, mock
          catalogues and frozen power analysis.</td><td>Stop any signature that cannot be recovered reliably.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Months 7&ndash;12</td><td>Preregistered training</td><td>Frozen hypotheses, priors, outcomes,
          exclusions and analysis code.</td><td>No new primary outcome after unblinding.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Months 13&ndash;16</td><td>Archival held-out test</td><td>Field-level predictive comparison and
          full null-injection report.</td><td>Proceed only if calibration and predictive gain both survive.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Months 17&ndash;22</td><td>Prospective follow-up</td><td>Published object-level forecasts and
          matched measurements.</td><td>A failed forecast cannot be replaced by a post-hoc one.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Months 23&ndash;24</td><td>Synthesis</td><td>Open catalogue, code, methods paper and decision
          report.</td><td>Support, restrict or reject the tested model.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame frame--creed">
    <p class="kicker">Decision rule</p>
    <h2 id="r-decision">What Would Count as Progress?</h2>
    <p><strong>Continue developing \(H_{-+}\)</strong> only if one conserved crossing model, fixed before
      unblinding, improves joint prediction in independent fields and its prospective object-level forecasts
      are calibrated. Even then, the result would identify an unexplained population law consistent with the
      model&mdash;not prove a previous universe.</p>
    <p><strong>Retain only \(H_{\chi}\)</strong> if a local integration law predicts the source&ndash;host
      sequence but no inherited-residue covariance or clock survives.</p>
    <p><strong>Prefer \(H_{\rm A}\)</strong> if conventional astrophysics matches or outpredicts the added
      models, if the crossing kernel cannot be derived, or if conservation requires hidden object-specific
      adjustments.</p>
    <p><strong>Reject the tested implementation</strong> if its prespecified redshift, covariance or energy
      predictions fail. A failed implementation need not disprove every imaginable cyclic cosmology; it does
      remove this version from scientific use.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap">
  <div class="page-head tight">
    <p class="kicker">People, tools, and outputs</p>
    <h2 id="r-resources">A Small Interdisciplinary Collaboration</h2>
  </div>
  <div class="grid three">
    <div class="frame card illuminated">
      <h3>Theory</h3>
      <p>Mathematical relativity, field theory and topology expertise to derive the seam map, dimensions and
        conservation law rather than infer them from the desired answer.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="frame card">
      <h3>Observation</h3>
      <p>Early-universe, AGN, stellar-population and radiative-transfer expertise to construct the baseline and
        prevent unfamiliar gas physics from being relabelled as new cosmology.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="frame card illuminated">
      <h3>Inference</h3>
      <p>Selection-function, simulation and predictive-model-comparison expertise, with an independent analyst
        responsible for the held-out evaluation.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p class="muted small">Phase I requires archival access, reproducible computing and staff time rather than
    immediate new observing time. A Phase II telescope proposal should be submitted only after the archival
    gate fixes the targets, measurements and required sensitivity.</p>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame">
    <p class="kicker">Observational foundation</p>
    <h2 id="r-sources">Starting Literature</h2>
    <p>The proposal begins from the evidence summarised in the parent conjecture: an early compact population,
      widespread signs of accreting black holes, dense ionised cocoons, at least one direct dynamical black-hole
      mass, and faint extended hosts in stacked imaging. These observations motivate the study but do not
      support its TTI extension by themselves.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="https://science.nasa.gov/missions/webb/newfound-galaxy-class-may-indicate-early-black-hole-growth-webb-finds/">NASA Webb: population census and accretion evidence</a></li>
      <li><a href="https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-025-09900-4">Rusakov et al.: young black holes in dense ionised cocoons</a></li>
      <li><a href="https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-026-10579-4">Juodžbalis et al.: direct Keplerian black-hole mass</a></li>
      <li><a href="https://www.nature.com/articles/s41550-026-02945-z">Zhang et al.: extended host components in stacked imaging</a></li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame frame--creed">
    <p class="lede">The proposal&rsquo;s standard is deliberately severe: TTI must predict a constrained relation
      that ordinary black-hole and galaxy physics did not already supply, and it must do so before the decisive
      observations are examined.</p>
